          "title": "Reasoning models reason well, until they don't",
          "url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22371",
          "source": "Hacker News",
          "date": "2025-10-31T09:23:41Z",
          "language": "en",
          "section": "us_news",
          "raw_content": "Large language models (LLMs) have shown significant progress in reasoning tasks. However, recent studies show that transformers and LLMs fail catastrophically once reasoning problems exceed modest complexity. We revisit these findings through the lens of large reasoning models (LRMs) -- LLMs fine-tuned with incentives for step-by-step argumentation and self-verification. LRM performance on graph and reasoning benchmarks such as NLGraph seem extraordinary, with some even claiming they are capable of generalized reasoning and innovation in reasoning-intensive fields such as mathematics, physics, medicine, and law. However, by more carefully scaling the complexity of reasoning problems, we show existing benchmarks actually have limited complexity. We develop a new dataset, the Deep Reasoning Dataset (DeepRD), along with a generative process for producing unlimited examples of scalable compl",
          "organization": "",
          "arxiv_id": "",
          "link_verified": true,
          "focus_match": [
            "ai-ml"
          ],
          "priority_hint": 7,
          "title_zh": "",
          "summary": "",
          "keywords": [],
          "is_highlight": false
        },
        {
          "title": "Ask HN: Who uses open LLMs and coding assistants locally? Share setup and laptop",
          "url": "https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=45771870",
          "source": "Hacker News",
          "date": "2025-10-31T13:39:55Z",
          "language": "en",
          "section": "us_news",
          "raw_content": "Dear Hackers, I’m interested in your real-world workflows for using open-source LLMs and open-source coding assistants on your laptop (not just cloud/enterprise SaaS). Specifically: Which model(s) are you running (e.g., Ollama, LM Studio, or others) and which open-source coding assistant/integration (for example, a VS Code plugin) you’re using? What laptop hardware do you have (CPU, GPU/NPU, memory, whether discrete GPU or integrated, OS) and how it performs for your workflow? What kinds of tasks you use it for (code completion, refactoring, debugging, code review) and how reliable it is (what works well / where it falls short). I'm conducting my own investigation, which I will be happy to share as well when over. Thanks! Andrea.",
          "organization": "",
